# Secrets and local configuration.
#
# Every `.env`-shaped file is ignored, not only the two currently in use: committing one real
# secret costs more than an over-broad pattern does. The negation is load-bearing —
# `.env.example` is the committed template, and `.env*` swallows it otherwise, leaving a fresh
# clone with nothing to copy. `.env.local` is written by `vercel link` and holds a short-lived
# OIDC token; the app reads neither file at runtime, since `api/index.py` hard-codes its
# configuration rather than inheriting it.
.env*
!.env.example

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# Test and tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Vercel project linkage — machine-local, and rewritten by `vercel link`.
.vercel/

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Demo captures (screen recording, screenshots) — generated, not source
/captures/

# Working notes, deliberately not part of the submission: the brief as it was handed over, my
# own planning notes, and scratch text. Named exactly, and anchored to the root, because the
# convenient pattern is the dangerous one here — a `*.md` or `*.txt` rule would swallow
# README.md and WRITEUP.md, which *are* the submission.
/Vibe Coder Recruitment.md
/BUILD_PLAN.md
/mailtext.txt
/TXT.txt

# Deliberately NOT ignored, because the thing that would break is not obvious from the name:
#
#   tests/data/rxnav_responses.json  the recorded RxNav payloads the offline suite replays.
#                                    The suite's claim is that it exercises RxNav's real
#                                    response shapes rather than assumptions about them, and a
#                                    clone without this file cannot run `make test` at all.
#   requirements.txt                 what Vercel installs the deployed function from.
#                                    `pyproject.toml` does not reach the build.
#   ui/index.html                    partly generated by scripts/build_ui_demo.py, but the
#                                    generated block is committed on purpose: tests/test_ui.py
#                                    asserts it still matches a fresh pipeline run, which is
#                                    what stops it becoming a second source of truth.
